In article <2782@tellab5.tellabs.com> seeya@tellabs.com (Mike Ciha) writes:
:I REALLY have to agree with Dave's discusstion about falling into pools.
:I mean where will it all end??
:
:You totter on the edge of a trap filled with poison-tipped spikes..
:Fall in (y/n)?
     I give thee two words: realism and strategy.  Both would prevent
such an absurdity.  To assume all logic but your own is arbitrary is
fallacious.  A movement in some direction is not a movement to the
extreme, just as the Eastern bloc countries aren't necessarily going to
end up in fascism simply because they're moving away from Communism.

:>confirmations belong in explore-mode where it is more appropriate to
:
:Yes, I agree whole-heartedly.  Careful play is what keeps you alive.
     And skill, by your system.  I might note that I consider changing
the explore-mode game too much defeats its purpose.  You don't want a
different game.  For instance, I only found out here that you only get a
"create which monster? [type the name]" prompt when in such a mode.  Why
didn't it act like it would in the real game?  That's certainly not
going to teach me to avoid cursed scrolls of create monster.  I think
the *only* difference should be the "Die? [yn]" prompt, and then of
course the keep save file option.

:A certain amount of randomness is what makes the game that much *more*
:interesting.
     Horrors!  Why not just toss a die at the beginning of the game and
tell you whether you won or not.  We can even make the game more
"difficult" by making it a d200 and only letting you win on a 1.  And,
of course, you'll have to wait two weeks between attempts to simulate
actual game play.
     Of course, I'm performing my own fallacy.  I'm not denouncing all
occurances of randomness.  But I'm of the opinion that at no point
should a random factor, except early in the game, cause unavoidable
death to the player.  (e.g. "You step on a death trap!")

:I suppose somebody at some point will request that a ^W (win game) option
:be put in........ :-)
     Would you be satisfied if it only worked 20% of the time and killed
the character otherwise?  I think not.  Nethack should be a game of
strategy, not luck or skill, I believe.

